# -*- mode:org; coding:utf-8 -*-
#+TITLE: cadgui-2-qt6-gui-host — the cadgui host: a Qt6 canvas on embedded ECL
#+STATUS: NEXT
#+PRIORITY: P3
#+TYPE: feature
#+DEPENDS: cadgui-1-ecl-port
#+TARGET: release 3.0
#+BRANCH: qt6-gui
#+DATE: 2026-11-16
#+PARENT: cadgui-release-3.0-epic

* Context

Phase 2 of [[cadgui-release-3.0-epic]]. Build the *cadgui* host: the graphical
peer of cadtui on the cador core, an interactive Qt6 canvas that renders the
cador model and feeds pointer/selection input back to =getpoint=/=entsel=/=ssget=
+ LISP =C:= commands, with pan/zoom. The smallest thing that looks like AutoCAD;
the foundation every later phase builds on.

* Architecture (DECIDED — in-process embedded ECL)

- *Shell*: a thin Qt6 Widgets C++ app is the host process; it =cl_boot='s embedded
  ECL and loads clautolisp. (Reuses =clautolisp/tools/clautolisp-gui-qt/=, which
  is already Qt6 Widgets.) Validated by =spike-ecl-embed/main.cpp=.
- *Boundary*: CL owns the model + regen and hands C++ a DISPLAY LIST; C++ owns the
  per-frame paint + pan/zoom + event capture; the boundary is crossed only on
  regen and on input events, NEVER per frame. A small C API (display-list pull +
  input supply + repaint request), registered into ECL via =ecl_def_c_function=.
  This in-process boundary REPLACES the former subprocess "protocol v2" sexp-wire
  sketch, and is the seed of the CRX ABI ([[cadgui-4-crx-api]]).
- *Input*: ONE thread, a NESTED QEventLoop. CL's =get*= blocks in a C function
  that runs a nested event loop until a =mousePressEvent= (or scripted click)
  supplies the value. No second thread => no GC/thread marriage. Validated by
  =spike-ecl-embed/interactive.cpp= (2000-point GC-stress clean).
- *Host*: =clautolisp --host cadgui= (established in the CLI, 2.4.21) installs the
  cadtui ui-tree on cador under the cadgui label; the =maybe-install-cadtui-host=
  =:cadgui= branch grows to start the Qt6 shell. cadgui REUSES cadtui's ui-tree
  classes, rendered graphically instead of textually.

* Threading architecture (pjb, 2026-11-17) -- THE constraint

Qt's event loop and ALL QWidget work MUST run on the process MAIN thread. The
cador runtime is multi-threaded (cador cooperative scheduler, "parking by
threads"):
- *2 threads per document* (lisp image): a RUNNER (runs the document's lisp) and
  a DRIVER (performs the blocking host request on its own thread and resumes the
  runner -- so a host request does not block the runner; this is the existing
  =%cador-maybe-park-read= / =scheduler-park= seam in prompt-api.lisp).
- *2 threads for the application* lisp image (same runner/driver pair).
- Qt may use its own threads too.

Consequence for the cadgui host: *Qt owns the main thread, so the application
lisp thread must be a NEW thread, NOT the main thread* (on the other hosts the
application lisp IS the main thread). The *MAIN thread runs the Qt event loop and
acts as the GUI driver*: when a document runner parks on
=getpoint=/=entsel=/=ssget=, the Qt main thread (its event loop) collects the
mouse point and RESUMES the parked runner (=scheduler-resume=) across threads.
Model mutations on a document/runner thread post a REGEN request to the Qt main
thread, which pulls a thread-safe display-list snapshot and repaints. All
QWidget access stays on the main thread; cross-thread hand-offs are thread-safe
(Qt queued/posted + the scheduler's park/resume).

NOTE -- the single-threaded case: the current shell + =input.lisp= run the lisp
and Qt on the SAME (main) thread with a NESTED QEventLoop inside getpoint. That
is the DEGENERATE single-document / no-scheduler case: there
=%cador-maybe-park-read= finds no scheduler and reads synchronously, so the
nested loop works and the live window is correct for ONE document. The
multi-threaded model above is the target once the cador scheduler + multiple
documents are in (shared with [[cadgui-3-2d-parity]] and the cooperative-
scheduler remainder). The input bridge is designed so both reduce to the same CL
seam (read-prompt-line / the input hook); only WHO blocks/resumes differs
(nested loop on one thread, vs park/resume across threads).

* Buildable slices (each implemented + tested + documented before the next)

- *A. cadgui module + host launch.* New =clautolisp/cadgui= system (reusing the
  cadtui ui-tree). =maybe-install-cadtui-host= =:cadgui= branch boots the Qt6
  shell with embedded ECL and an empty modelspace.
  TEST: host-install fiveam (the =:cadgui= branch builds the tree + sets the
  label, already partly covered); OFFSCREEN smoke -- the shell boots, grabs a PNG
  (the spike proves the mechanism).
- *B. Scene exporter (CL, host-agnostic).* Walk the current space (the
  =host-ssget "X"= loop) and tessellate each =entity-handle= =data= alist to
  world-space polylines: LINE->segment, LW/POLYLINE->segments, CIRCLE/ARC/ELLIPSE
  ->polyline (reuse =cador/command-geometry.lisp= / =intersect.lisp=),
  TEXT/MTEXT->baseline box, INSERT->block bbox; tag handle + layer + ACI. Emit a
  display list.
  TEST: fiveam fixture-drawing assertions on the emitted display list. No Qt.
- *C. Qt6 canvas widget.* Render the display list in ACI colours (reuse =aciToRgb=)
  over grid + axes + crosshair; pan (middle-drag), zoom (wheel, cursor-anchored),
  zoom-extents; a command-line =QLineEdit=. World<->device transform in C++.
  TEST: OFFSCREEN golden-image / pixel-sanity on a fixture display list; the
  transform math unit-tested.
- *D. Input bridge.* While a =get*=/=entsel=/=ssget= is pending, put the canvas in
  the matching mode; convert the =mousePressEvent= (point / pick via =%pick-entity=
  / window via =%window-selection=) to the value cador's =read-prompt-line=
  expects, via the nested event loop. Makes bare =(getpoint)= / =(entsel)= /
  =(ssget)= + LISP =C:= commands work from the mouse unchanged.
  TEST: SCRIPTED nested-loop tests (spike #2 pattern) headless: a scripted click
  satisfies =(getpoint)=; a scripted pick returns the ename; a scripted window
  returns the pickset.
- *E. Command line + regen-on-change.* =(:command STRING)= evaluates on the
  =repl-loop= path; after each top-level eval that changes =dbmod=, re-export the
  scene and repaint. Coarse "regen after every form" first.
  TEST: end-to-end headless -- a scripted command draws a line that appears in the
  re-exported display list; golden image of the result.
- *F. The C boundary, named + typed.* Formalise the display-list + input +
  repaint C API as the stable in-process surface (the CRX seed): a versioned,
  documented set of entry points, not ad-hoc =cl_safe_eval= strings.
  TEST: a C-level round-trip test (display list out, input in) exercised headless.

* Scope — IN (phase 2)

The five slices above: an interactive single-canvas window on the embedded-ECL
cadgui host, with =getpoint=/=entsel=/=ssget= + LISP commands driving from the
mouse, pan/zoom, coarse regen, offscreen-capable.

* Scope — OUT (named, so the boundary is honest)

- Live "click-to-draw with the BUILT-IN command" (wire =%cmd-*= to park on canvas
  input): that is phase-2-increment-2 / [[cadgui-3-2d-parity]]. Phase 2 delivers
  interactive input via =get*=/=entsel=/=ssget= + LISP commands, enough to prove
  the loop end to end.
- Rubber-band / drag preview, grips, osnap, tracking, dynamic input -> phase 3.
- 3D: ortho top view only, Z ignored; no visual styles/OpenGL -> phases 5-6.
- Fidelity: no true glyphs (box), no hatch fill (boundary only), no linetypes/
  lineweights, no dimension rendering -> phase 3.
- No DWG open/save from the canvas UI (load via CLI/AutoLISP); no app shell
  (ribbon, palettes, layout tabs) -> phase 3.
- =nentsel= -- not in cador yet; out.

* Acceptance criteria

- [ ] =clautolisp --host cadgui= opens a Qt6 window (or, headless, renders
      offscreen): empty modelspace, grid, axes, crosshair; pan/zoom/extents work
      LOCALLY with no Lisp round-trip.
- [ ] =(command "LINE" "0,0" "10,10" "")= (REPL or canvas line) draws a line that
      appears after regen.
- [ ] =(getpoint)= -> a click returns the world point; =(entsel)= -> clicking a
      line returns its ename and highlights it; =(ssget)= window/crossing returns
      the enclosed/crossed pickset.
- [ ] A sample LISP =C:= command calling =getpoint= + =entsel= runs end to end
      from the mouse.
- [ ] A fixture drawing (LINE/CIRCLE/ARC/LWPOLYLINE/TEXT-box/INSERT-bbox) renders
      correctly in ortho top view.
- [ ] CI: slices A/B/D/E/F covered by headless Lisp + offscreen tests (no live
      display); the live-window run is a manual/gui lane, as the DCL Qt tool is.
- [ ] Every canvas interaction is registered as an Action-Layer action with a
      headless API test (the driveability guard, [[cadgui-action-layer]]).

* Documentation plan

- =clautolisp/cadgui/documentation/clautolisp-cadgui-spec.org= -- the host spec,
  grown per slice (boundary, display list, input modes, regen).
- A cadgui user-manual section (invocation =--host cadgui=, canvas controls).
- =--list-hosts= already names cadgui; keep its one-liner current.

* Status

- DONE: Qt-API decision; two spikes; =--host cadgui= CLI identity (2.4.21).
- DONE: slices *A/B/C/F* (2.4.22) -- the =clautolisp/cadgui= module + the
  =cador-scene-display-list= exporter + the embedded-ECL Qt6 shell rendering a
  real engine-built scene offscreen. cador suite + cadgui suite green.
- DONE: slices *D/E* (2.4.23) -- the GUI input bridge (=cadgui-input-stream=
  backs cador's prompt-stream; getpoint/entsel drive from canvas clicks through
  =read-prompt-line=) and =cadgui-eval-command= (AutoLISP commands draw via the
  real engine). cadgui suite 28/28; full aggregate 13837/0. The shell demo draws
  a line between two getpoint'd canvas clicks, end to end.
- DONE: the *LIVE interactive window* (single-document) -- =cadgui-shell.cpp= is
  now a real app (=QApplication::exec=): a pan/zoom =View= transform (cursor-
  anchored wheel, middle-drag pan, =e= zoom-extents), a crosshair while picking,
  real left-click -> device->world -> getpoint/entsel, and a command-line widget
  that evals AutoLISP and repaints. Modes: live / =--render FILE= / =--demo FILE=.
  The device->world + getpoint path is validated headless via =--demo= (synthetic
  clicks through the REAL mousePressEvent draw a line between the clicked points).
- DOCUMENTED: the *threading architecture* (above) -- Qt on the main thread, the
  application lisp on a NEW thread, document runners parked/resumed across threads
  by the Qt-main-thread driver. The current shell is the degenerate single-
  document / no-scheduler case.
- VALIDATED (spike, 2026-11-17): the MULTI-THREADED FOUNDATION --
  =spike-ecl-embed/mt-threads.cpp=. =cl_boot= + Qt's event loop on the MAIN
  thread; the application lisp runs on a NEW ECL thread
  (=mp:process-run-function=). The runner blocks cross-thread in a C
  =mt-await-point= on a thread-safe =PointQueue= the main thread fills (the
  cross-thread getpoint, no nested loop -- the runner just blocks while Qt keeps
  running on main); it mutates a CL model under =mp:with-lock=, which the main
  thread reads under the same lock to paint; it requests repaints cross-thread
  via =QMetaObject::invokeMethod(..., Qt::QueuedConnection)=; and it cons-storms
  for GC stress throughout. Result: =runner_done=1 segments=5=, the 5 segments
  drawn between the 6 supplied points render correctly offscreen, no
  crash/deadlock. This de-risks the whole ECL-threads + Qt-on-main + shared-CL-
  model marriage -- the core the real multi-threaded host is built on.
- DONE (2026-11-17): the MULTI-THREADED live window, single document. The real
  =cadgui-shell.cpp= now runs the pattern on the real cador engine: Qt's event
  loop on the MAIN thread; the application lisp on a NEW ECL thread
  (=mp:process-run-function "cadgui-app"=); the command line posts AutoLISP
  strings on a =CommandQueue=; a canvas click posts a world point on a
  =PointQueue= that the runner's blocked =getpoint=/=entsel= consumes through
  =*cadgui-input-hook*= / =read-prompt-line=; the runner is the SINGLE actor
  touching the model (it evals AND exports the display list, so no cross-thread
  CL race and no cador-wide lock); the main thread paints only a =std::mutex=-
  guarded C++ snapshot the runner publishes; repaint is requested cross-thread
  via =QMetaObject::invokeMethod(Queued)=. Validated headless end to end
  (=--demo=): runner takes the command, getpoint #1 and #2 are each satisfied by
  a cross-thread canvas click, =entmake= draws through the real engine, the
  6-prim snapshot is published and rendered, clean quit. (A feeder deadlock --
  polling a sub-millisecond awaiting edge -- was fixed by counting getpoint
  REQUESTS and feeding one click per request.)
- DONE (2026-11-17): ECL workflow to AVOID RELOADING clautolisp each run
  (integrates =~/src/public/rules/skills/ecl=; see =shell/NOTES-ecl.md=).
  Measured here: a fresh embedded =cl_boot= + =asdf:load-system= costs ~96 s
  (cold) EVERY run, because this ECL cannot reuse a WARM fasl cache --
  =load-system= against a populated cache overflows the C stack in the planner
  (=ASDF/PLAN::STAMP=), under BOTH Quicklisp's ASDF 3.3.7.3 and ECL's bundled
  3.1.8.8. And this Debian ECL ships NO static libs (=libcmp.a=/=libecl.a=
  absent), so image-op/program-op (dev image, standalone exe) cannot LINK here.
  The approach that works: a monolithic STATIC LIBRARY (=build-cadgui-lib.sh= ->
  =clautolisp--cadgui--all-systems.a=, just =ar=, no =libcmp.a=) linked into the
  shell (=CADGUI_USE_LIB=1 bash build.sh=) and initialized from C with
  =ecl_init_module= -- no runtime ASDF, so no overflow and no recompile.
  MEASURED: frozen startup ~1.76 s vs ~96 s (~55x). =cadgui-dev.asd= +
  =build-dev-image.{lisp,sh}= are the EC10 dev-image recipe for Lisp iteration,
  kept for environments with static libs (source-built ECL / CI image).
- DONE (2026-11-17, 2.4.24): ssget WINDOW/CROSSING (two-point) through the
  input bridge. The engine already supports =(ssget "W"/"C" p1 p2)=
  (selection-api.lisp); the cadgui path reads BOTH corners with =(getpoint)= fed
  by the canvas, so =(ssget "W" (getpoint) (getpoint))= / "C" select the
  enclosed / crossed entities. Covered by two cadgui-suite tests
  (protocol-tests.lisp): a window selects a fully-enclosed line; a line crossing
  the window boundary is EXCLUDED by "W" but SELECTED by "C" (observed via an
  erase of the selected member). cadgui suite 31/31. (Bare interactive =(ssget)=
  with rubber-band auto window/crossing stays phase 3.)
- NEXT: per-document runner/driver threads + cross-thread park/resume via the
  cador scheduler (shared with the cooperative-scheduler remainder);
  regen-on-dbmod (dbmod is a sticky bitfield, not monotonic -- needs a cheap
  monotonic regen serial in cador, or compare the exported list); entsel
  highlight (needs the picked handle surfaced to the shell); registering every
  canvas interaction through [[cadgui-action-layer]]. Then
  [[cadgui-3-2d-parity]].
